"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7.8. The surgical findings included a high-risk profile: Gleason 4+5 (ISUP grade group 5), positive surgical margins, invasion of the seminal vesicles, and lymphovascular invasion, although no lymph node metastasis was noted. The biopsy revealed a Gleason 3+4 pattern, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the presence of seminal vesicle invasion. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the trajectory of recurrence. The current horizon suggests a potential event within the calculated range. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent changes would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 29.84 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 23.87–35.81 months; its coverage has not been validated."
